 lost to me unknowingly simply because  somebody could not remember pronounce or  spell my name  what other things were lost  so it is since then i became emboldened  daily in both quiet and loud ways for  advocates for now what i call global  nominal equity  meaning we should treat each name with  the respect that it deserves and we make  the appropriate accommodations that we  pronounce and spell everyone's name  always everywhere  well if you haven't heard of global  nominal equity before you're not alone  because i just took the creative license  to make it up a couple months ago  and now it's our turn i'm sharing it  with you so that we can co-own it  co-coin it and co-create it together  well  my life has been structured by my name  for a very long time like actually all  the rest of you in the audience  on a good day somebody who meets me for  the first time will say how do i  pronounce your name i will say chingus  with a smile they will say ching is with  a smile and they will continue to  pronounce it without hesitation with a  smile in their eyes with positive  feelings all around  on a bad day however  somebody who meets me for the first time  will say how do i pronounce your name  i will say chingus  and they will say  ah this sounds very difficult i think i  will butcher it so i'm scared i'm just  not gonna say it and then i'll say oh  it's not that difficult come on well  it's two syllables actually it's chin  and giz there's plenty of wobbles and  i'll even sprinkle in a historical  connection by saying it comes from  genghis khan so that it gets anchored in  your mind well their answer is the bad  they remember their answer continues as  saying uh first of all who is that i  don't know and second of all uh it's  really difficult i'll just pass this one  out  to just proceed to interact with me  without mentioning my name ever again  and potentially forgetting many things  that we talked about except one small  fact that my name was unpronounceable  well this doesn't only happen to me in  the broader anglo-saxon universe it also  happens to melee evgeny agnieszka deidra  and adabawale  but we are all perpetrators of this the  number of times i said margot instead of  margot  or genie instead of jean before i was  corrected that he's not a character from  aladdin  was just one too many  and that is why  i believe  that we should make sure that we do not  provide blatant commentary on the most  personal and intimate yet public thing  that we all have are first names  that is exactly why today i chose to  talk to you about the notion of having  an unpronounceable names  and what should we do to ensure that we  treat each name respectfully and make  the appropriate accommodations  one's name  the sound of which the spelling of which  the shape of every letter of which to  dramatize a little bit has a direct  impact on our existence  our self-ideation our public perception  our chances of leaving a legacy our past  and our future  many people we know of went to great  lengths to change their names so that  they could make them more pronounceable  isur danielovic became kirk douglas  natalie herschlag became natalie portman  tint woods became tiger woods  and robert allen zimmerman became bob  dylan and he in fact famously said some  people are just born with the wrong  names  i disagree with them too after  disagreeing with shakespeare  well  actually  research has shown many things about  this front research shows five things  one that people with unpronounceable  names have more difficulty forming  intimate connections  they have more difficulty too they have  more difficulty last leaving a lasting  impression during a job interview  three they have difficulty  having their name being remembered  four they've performed worse at school  and five they have a smaller chances of  being an elected public official  yes even that  unpronounceable names are more likely to  be misremembered and mispronounced even  after correction  and with each  mispronunciation slightly altering our  self-perceived identity  our already complicated existence  our foreignness and our difference  and that is why  it is up to our hands to ensure  that we do not introduce further micro  inequalities into our already deeply  unequal world  and take up the matter into our hands  and find a way in which we treat  everyone with a challenging or an easy  name with the same willingness to  remember  excitement and curiosity  well you might ask how do we do that  it actually starts  very simple with that very very simple  shift it's a mental shift  it starts with realizing one thing  and that is  that no name is actually unpronounceable  it's a little bit duh but it is the  there are no names that are  unpronounceable  if somebody chose to name their child  something this means they were able to  pronounce it  that also means you too after not too  many attempts will be able to pronounce  it  i promise if you cannot pronounce it  right they will correct it  the key is to lean into it not to shy  away from it  there's a lot of shame in not trying but  there's no shame in trying  so the next time when you come across a  name that you immediately deem to be  relatively complicated and  unpronounceable  take a second  think about it in your mind rehearse it  and then say it out loud and then ask  the person did i say it right if you  didn't they will correct it and after  not too many attempts you will  ultimately get it right  well it's sometimes a little bit more  difficult so what you'll have to do my  recommendation is that lean into it a  bit more and then try to use the name  actually refer to the person with the  name without omitting it so every time  you're actually trying to get that  person's attention say the name again  and again and again so that it gets  anchored in your mind and for bonus  points you can even ask what it means  and where it comes from  well it is time  that we make every name pronounceable  not by changing our names but by  changing our attitudes towards them  we need to ensure that we live in a  world in which everybody feels equally  seen and equally respected  it is time that we the ones with  unpronounceable names  have to stop carrying the burden of  others inability to pronounce our names  so to build that equitable world let's  all commit ourselves to making every  word that seemingly unpronounceable  pronounceable and with that every person  that is seemingly foreign intricately  familiar  and to do so let's commit ourselves to  now our co-owned and co-coined term  global nominal equity  thank you  [Music]  you

